        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Common law robbery is the trespassory taking and carrying away of the personal property of another from the person or immediate presence of another, by force or the threat of immediate physical force (intimidation/fear), with the intent to permanently deprive the owner thereof. Threatening to shoot the cashier while pointing a concealed finger shaped like a gun creates immediate reasonable apprehension and fear of lethal violence, fully satisfying the intimidation requirement for common law robbery. However, regarding the statutory offense of armed robbery, under strict traditional definitions, the defendant must in fact be armed with an actual dangerous or deadly weapon; feigning a weapon with an index finger or pocket protrusion does not satisfy the armed element (in the absence of an explicit statute defining simulated weapons as armed robbery). Because Defendant was completely unarmed, armed robbery fails, making common law robbery the highest offense committed (Option B). Option A is incorrect because Defendant was not armed with an actual dangerous or deadly weapon. Option C is incorrect because larceny by trick involves obtaining possession through fraud or deceit without force or fear, whereas Defendant obtained property through active threats of imminent death (robbery). Option D is incorrect because extortion (blackmail) requires obtaining property through threats of future harm, not threats of immediate physical violence."
